1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,718
Ava: What if moving a PyTorch training loop to a different AI chip took one device change?
Ava：如果只改一个设备设置，就能把 PyTorch 训练循环迁移到另一种 AI 芯片上呢？

2
00:00:05,718 --> 00:00:08,894
That could save a lot of engineering time.
那能省下大量工程时间。

3
00:00:08,894 --> 00:00:11,650
Brian: That's the promise behind TorchNeuron.
Brian：这正是 TorchNeuron 的目标。

4
00:00:11,650 --> 00:00:18,964
It makes AWS Trainium, a family of chips for AI training and inference, a native PyTorch device.
它让用于 AI 训练和推理的 AWS Trainium 芯片成为 PyTorch 原生设备。

5
00:00:18,964 --> 00:00:25,170
Ava: Why does native support matter? People have already been running models on Trainium.
Ava：原生支持为什么重要？大家不是已经能在 Trainium 上运行模型了吗？

6
00:00:25,170 --> 00:00:29,910
Brian: They have. But models, libraries, and training tools keep changing.
Brian：没错，但模型、库和训练工具一直在变。

7
00:00:29,910 --> 00:00:36,416
Teams want to use familiar PyTorch workflows without adapting their software each time.
团队希望沿用熟悉的 PyTorch 工作流程，不必每次都调整软件。

8
00:00:36,416 --> 00:00:42,670
Ava: So the cost isn't just how long a model runs. It's also the work of getting it running.
Ava：所以成本不只是模型运行时间，还包括让它跑起来的工作。

9
00:00:42,670 --> 00:00:43,094
Brian: Right.
Brian：对。

10
00:00:43,094 --> 00:00:51,996
The article includes the time spent making a workload work, tuning it, and maintaining it as models and libraries evolve.
文章还把适配工作负载、调优，以及随模型和库演进进行维护的时间算进去。

11
00:00:51,996 --> 00:00:55,538
Ava: What changed in PyTorch to make this possible?
Ava：PyTorch 有什么变化，才让这成为可能？

12
00:00:55,538 --> 00:00:59,023
Brian: PyTorch made many APIs accelerator-agnostic.
Brian：PyTorch 让许多 API 不再依赖特定加速器。

13
00:00:59,023 --> 00:01:04,014
That means code can address a device through common interfaces.
这样，代码就能通过通用接口使用设备。

14
00:01:04,014 --> 00:01:08,800
It also added official ways to register custom hardware.
它还提供了注册自定义硬件的官方方式。

15
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:12,174
Ava: And TorchNeuron uses one of those ways?
Ava：TorchNeuron 用的就是其中一种方式？

16
00:01:12,174 --> 00:01:17,959
Brian: Yes. PrivateUse1 is PyTorch's extension point for a custom device backend.
Brian：是的。PrivateUse1 是 PyTorch 为自定义设备后端提供的扩展点。

17
00:01:17,959 --> 00:01:23,468
TorchNeuron registers Trainium there under the device name neuron.
TorchNeuron 通过它注册 Trainium，设备名为 neuron。

18
00:01:23,468 --> 00:01:28,762
Ava: Give me the simplest example. What does the developer actually change?
Ava：举个最简单的例子。开发者实际要改什么？

19
00:01:28,762 --> 00:01:33,626
Brian: In the article's training loop, they move the model to the neuron device.
Brian：在文章的训练循环中，只需把模型移到 neuron 设备上。

20
00:01:33,626 --> 00:01:38,640
The optimizer and the rest of that loop stay in familiar PyTorch form.
优化器和循环的其余部分仍是熟悉的 PyTorch 写法。

21
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:44,774
Ava: That's pleasantly boring. Does device-independent code need even that change?
Ava：这倒是省心。设备无关的代码连这一步都不用改吗？

22
00:01:44,774 --> 00:01:53,617
Brian: Code already written against torch dot accelerator, PyTorch's recommended device-agnostic API, can run unmodified.
Brian：如果代码已使用 PyTorch 推荐的设备无关 API torch.accelerator，就能直接运行，无需修改。

23
00:01:53,617 --> 00:01:58,156
That's useful when a project supports several devices.
这对支持多种设备的项目很有用。

24
00:01:58,156 --> 00:02:03,402
Ava: Okay, the model is on Trainium. How does an operation run?
Ava：好，模型已经放到 Trainium 上了。运算怎么执行？

25
00:02:03,402 --> 00:02:08,621
Brian: There are two main modes: eager execution and torch dot compile.
Brian：主要有两种模式：即时执行和 torch.compile。

26
00:02:08,621 --> 00:02:12,290
Eager runs operations as Python reaches them.
即时执行会在 Python 运行到某个运算时执行它。

27
00:02:12,290 --> 00:02:16,880
Compile captures work into a graph for optimization.
编译模式则把运算捕获成计算图，以便优化。

28
00:02:16,880 --> 00:02:22,102
Ava: So eager is the mode where I can still print values and use breakpoints?
Ava：所以即时执行模式还能打印数值、设置断点？

29
00:02:22,102 --> 00:02:25,440
Brian: Exactly. Python control flow works there too.
Brian：没错，Python 的控制流也能正常工作。

30
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:32,244
That's why the article presents eager as the natural choice for research and debugging.
因此，文章认为即时执行很适合研究和调试。

31
00:02:32,244 --> 00:02:36,770
Ava: But running every operation separately sounds expensive.
Ava：但逐个执行运算听起来开销很大。

32
00:02:36,770 --> 00:02:37,529
Brian: It can be.
Brian：确实可能如此。

33
00:02:37,529 --> 00:02:45,878
Each eager operation compiles its own Neuron Executable File Format file, or NEFF, and launches on the device.
每个即时执行的运算都会单独编译成 Neuron 可执行文件格式文件（NEFF），再在设备上启动。

34
00:02:45,878 --> 00:02:49,720
Intermediate data moves through device memory.
中间数据通过设备内存传递。

35
00:02:49,720 --> 00:02:52,830
Ava: Is that why there's an adaptive eager mode?
Ava：所以才有自适应即时执行模式？

36
00:02:52,830 --> 00:02:54,572
Brian: Yes. It's experimental.
Brian：对，它还处于实验阶段。

37
00:02:54,572 --> 00:03:04,388
The goal is to reduce launch cost and create more chances to fuse operations, while keeping the interactive eager experience.
目标是在保持即时执行交互体验的同时，降低启动开销，并增加运算融合的机会。

38
00:03:04,388 --> 00:03:09,202
Ava: Fuse as in combine several small jobs into one larger job?
Ava：融合，就是把几个小任务合成一个大任务？

39
00:03:09,202 --> 00:03:14,025
Brian: That's the idea. Imagine carrying groceries one item at a time.
Brian：正是如此。想象一下每次只拿一件东西搬运杂货。

40
00:03:14,025 --> 00:03:20,040
Combining trips can help, and you still get to work in an interactive way.
合并几趟搬运会更省事，同时还能保持交互式的工作方式。

41
00:03:20,040 --> 00:03:23,438
Ava: Then when would I choose torch dot compile?
Ava：那什么时候该选 torch.compile？

42
00:03:23,438 --> 00:03:27,430
Brian: For a stable model where production performance matters.
Brian：模型已经稳定，而且生产环境的性能很重要时。

43
00:03:27,430 --> 00:03:35,620
It captures a graph of operations, lets the Neuron compiler optimize it, and produces a NEFF for the hardware.
它捕获运算图，交给 Neuron 编译器优化，再生成供硬件运行的 NEFF。

44
00:03:35,620 --> 00:03:38,394
Ava: Do I have to compile the whole model?
Ava：必须编译整个模型吗？

45
00:03:38,394 --> 00:03:46,520
Brian: No. The article says you can optimize all or part of it, and compiled work can run alongside eager work.
Brian：不用。文章说可以优化全部或部分模型，编译后的运算也能与即时执行的运算并行使用。

46
00:03:46,520 --> 00:03:51,502
Ava: What happens between PyTorch code and that final device file?
Ava：从 PyTorch 代码到最终的设备文件，中间经历了什么？

47
00:03:51,502 --> 00:03:57,192
Brian: TorchDynamo captures an FX graph, a graph representation of the model.
Brian：TorchDynamo 会捕获 FX 图，也就是模型的图表示。

48
00:03:57,192 --> 00:04:01,419
AOTAutograd traces the backward graph ahead of time.
AOTAutograd 会提前追踪反向传播图。

49
00:04:01,419 --> 00:04:06,972
Torch-MLIR and StableHLO sit on the path to the Neuron compiler.
Torch-MLIR 和 StableHLO 位于通往 Neuron 编译器的处理流程中。

50
00:04:06,972 --> 00:04:11,786
Ava: That's a lot of stages. What's the useful picture to keep in mind?
Ava：步骤真不少。该怎样简单理解整个过程？

51
00:04:11,786 --> 00:04:21,699
Brian: PyTorch gathers the work, prepares the forward and backward paths, then passes them through compiler stages until Trainium can execute them.
Brian：PyTorch 汇集计算任务，准备前向和反向路径，再经过各编译阶段，最终交给 Trainium 执行。

52
00:04:21,699 --> 00:04:23,536
That's the key point.
这就是关键。

53
00:04:23,536 --> 00:04:27,318
Ava: And how does someone select the Neuron compiler?
Ava：那怎么选择 Neuron 编译器？

54
00:04:27,318 --> 00:04:33,760
Brian: They can set neuron as the default backend once, or name it in a torch dot compile call.
Brian：可以一次性将 neuron 设为默认后端，也可以在调用 torch.compile 时指定它。

55
00:04:33,760 --> 00:04:36,380
The article shows both choices.
文章介绍了这两种方式。

56
00:04:36,380 --> 00:04:42,970
Ava: Does native support reach normal PyTorch features, or just model execution?
Ava：原生支持涵盖常用的 PyTorch 功能，还是只支持模型执行？

57
00:04:42,970 --> 00:04:52,547
Brian: It reaches familiar interfaces for tensors, neural network modules, automatic mixed precision, distributed work, and profiling.
Brian：张量、神经网络模块、自动混合精度、分布式计算和性能分析，都能使用熟悉的接口。

58
00:04:52,547 --> 00:04:56,592
The article also reports broad operator coverage.
文章还提到，它支持大量算子。

59
00:04:56,592 --> 00:05:01,334
Ava: Broad sounds useful, but it doesn't mean every operator.
Ava：支持大量算子是好事，但也不是支持所有算子。

60
00:05:01,334 --> 00:05:03,496
Brian: Correct. We'll get to the gaps.
Brian：没错。稍后会讲到哪些还不支持。

61
00:05:03,496 --> 00:05:12,844
For supported models, the article lists common floating-point types, integer and boolean types, and working mixed precision code.
对于受支持的模型，文章列出了常见浮点、整数和布尔类型，以及可正常运行的混合精度代码。

62
00:05:12,844 --> 00:05:16,578
Ava: What about training across many devices?
Ava：在多台设备上训练呢？

63
00:05:16,578 --> 00:05:20,393
Brian: The standard distributed APIs are supported.
Brian：标准的分布式 API 也受支持。

64
00:05:20,393 --> 00:05:29,672
That includes DDP, or Distributed Data Parallel, and FSDP2, or Fully Sharded Data Parallel version two.
包括 DDP（分布式数据并行）和 FSDP2（完全分片数据并行第二版）。

65
00:05:29,672 --> 00:05:34,846
Ava: Can people combine those ideas with tensor and pipeline parallelism?
Ava：这些方法能和张量并行、流水线并行结合吗？

66
00:05:34,846 --> 00:05:35,162
Brian: Yes.
Brian：能。

67
00:05:35,162 --> 00:05:44,559
The article describes tensor parallelism, pipeline parallelism, and combinations such as FSDP2 with tensor parallelism.
文章介绍了张量并行、流水线并行，以及 FSDP2 与张量并行等组合。

68
00:05:44,559 --> 00:05:49,884
DTensor and DeviceMesh help manage sharding and process groups.
DTensor 和 DeviceMesh 有助于管理分片和进程组。

69
00:05:49,884 --> 00:05:53,546
Ava: And the devices still need to exchange data.
Ava：设备之间还是得交换数据。

70
00:05:53,546 --> 00:05:59,702
Brian: Right. Collective operations include gathering and reducing data across workers.
Brian：对。集合通信操作包括在各工作进程之间收集和归约数据。

71
00:05:59,702 --> 00:06:04,473
Within a node, they use Trainium's dedicated collective cores.
在节点内部，它们使用 Trainium 专用的集合通信核心。

72
00:06:04,473 --> 00:06:08,440
Between nodes, they use Elastic Fabric Adapter.
在节点之间，则使用 Elastic Fabric Adapter。

73
00:06:08,440 --> 00:06:12,102
Ava: What if a project uses streams to overlap work?
Ava：如果项目用流来重叠执行任务呢？

74
00:06:12,102 --> 00:06:17,702
Brian: Streams and events are supported. A stream keeps its own operations ordered.
Brian：流和事件都受支持。每条流内的操作按顺序执行。

75
00:06:17,702 --> 00:06:24,020
If one stream needs another stream's result, you record an event and wait for it.
如果一条流需要另一条流的结果，就记录一个事件并等待它。

76
00:06:24,020 --> 00:06:27,946
Ava: Like handing over a ticket before the next worker starts?
Ava：就像把凭证交给下一位工作人员，他才能开工？

77
00:06:27,946 --> 00:06:28,534
Brian: Exactly.
Brian：正是。

78
00:06:28,534 --> 00:06:34,410
There's a hardware detail, though: non-default streams share one hardware queue.
不过有个硬件细节：非默认流共用一条硬件队列。

79
00:06:34,410 --> 00:06:40,416
They keep ordering and event behavior, but don't each get a dedicated engine.
它们仍保持操作顺序和事件机制，但并非每条流都有独立的执行单元。

80
00:06:40,416 --> 00:06:47,054
Ava: So the PyTorch behavior is there, but I shouldn't picture a separate engine for every stream.
Ava：所以 PyTorch 的行为是一样的，但不能以为每条流都有独立的执行单元。

81
00:06:47,054 --> 00:06:52,886
Brian: You've got it. And an asynchronous error appears at the next synchronization point.
Brian：理解对了。异步错误会在下一个同步点显现。

82
00:06:52,886 --> 00:06:59,068
TorchNeuron tracks the earliest exception and reports it with its original context.
TorchNeuron 会追踪最早出现的异常，并连同原始上下文一起报告。

83
00:06:59,068 --> 00:07:04,181
Ava: Suppose the standard operations aren't fast enough for one critical layer.
Ava：假如某个关键层用标准操作还不够快呢？

84
00:07:04,181 --> 00:07:05,706
What's available?
有什么办法？

85
00:07:05,706 --> 00:07:13,777
Brian: NKI, the Neuron Kernel Interface, lets developers write custom operators with lower-level hardware control.
Brian：NKI（Neuron Kernel Interface）让开发者能编写自定义算子，更直接地控制底层硬件。

86
00:07:13,777 --> 00:07:18,200
Those operators can work in eager and compiled models.
这些算子可用于即时执行和编译后的模型。

87
00:07:18,200 --> 00:07:22,654
Ava: Does that mean rewriting the entire model as custom kernels?
Ava：这意味着要把整个模型重写成自定义内核吗？

88
00:07:22,654 --> 00:07:27,993
Brian: No. The article suggests using NKI for performance-critical pieces.
Brian：不用。文章建议只在性能关键的部分使用 NKI。

89
00:07:27,993 --> 00:07:37,428
It also describes NKI-lib, an open-source set of optimized operators for common language and multimodal workloads.
文章还介绍了 NKI-lib：一套面向常见语言和多模态工作负载的开源优化算子。

90
00:07:37,428 --> 00:07:42,026
Ava: There's a sine example in the article. What should I take from it?
Ava：文章里有个正弦函数示例。它说明了什么？

91
00:07:42,026 --> 00:07:50,996
Brian: It registers a custom sine operator and its backward calculation using PyTorch's recommended autograd registration style.
Brian：它按照 PyTorch 推荐的 autograd 注册方式，注册了自定义正弦算子及其反向计算。

92
00:07:50,996 --> 00:07:54,904
The forward and backward paths call NKI kernels.
前向和反向路径都会调用 NKI 内核。

93
00:07:54,904 --> 00:07:59,550
Ava: Nice. Once it's running, how do I find the slow part?
Ava：不错。跑起来后，怎么找出慢在哪里？

94
00:07:59,550 --> 00:08:06,384
Brian: TorchNeuron extends the standard torch dot profiler interface with Neuron-specific settings.
Brian：TorchNeuron 在标准的 torch.profiler 接口上增加了 Neuron 专用设置。

95
00:08:06,384 --> 00:08:12,380
It can collect framework, runtime, and device activity in a connected trace.
它能把框架、运行时和设备活动采集到一条关联的追踪记录中。

96
00:08:12,380 --> 00:08:13,344
Ava: Connected how?
Ava：怎么关联？

97
00:08:13,344 --> 00:08:19,546
Profiling tools sometimes give me three timelines that don't seem to know each other.
性能分析工具有时给我三条时间线，看起来彼此毫无关联。

98
00:08:19,546 --> 00:08:24,368
Brian: Neuron Explorer links events across those layers and back to source lines.
Brian：Neuron Explorer 能把这些层面的事件关联起来，并追溯到源代码行。

99
00:08:24,368 --> 00:08:31,104
It also shows call hierarchy and dependency chains, so you can inspect what a stall is waiting on.
它还会显示调用层级和依赖链，方便查看停顿时在等待什么。

100
00:08:31,104 --> 00:08:36,422
Ava: Can an engineer analyze the data without the visual interface?
Ava：工程师能不通过可视化界面分析这些数据吗？

101
00:08:36,422 --> 00:08:36,704
Brian: Yes.
Brian：对。

102
00:08:36,704 --> 00:08:46,780
Neuron Explorer emits Parquet metrics for operations and engines, and the built-in profiler tools can sort operations by device-side cost.
Neuron Explorer 会生成记录算子和引擎指标的 Parquet 文件，内置分析工具还能按设备端开销对算子排序。

103
00:08:46,780 --> 00:08:52,002
Ava: Let's move from features to evidence. What has the team tested?
Ava：从功能说到证据吧。团队测试了什么？

104
00:08:52,002 --> 00:08:57,065
Brian: They report a continuous sweep of one hundred thousand Hugging Face models.
Brian：他们称持续测试了10万个 Hugging Face 模型。

105
00:08:57,065 --> 00:09:03,896
Based on download counts, those models cover ninety-nine point nine percent of real-world usage.
按下载量计算，这些模型覆盖了99.9%的实际使用量。

106
00:09:03,896 --> 00:09:06,670
Ava: What did they check for each model?
Ava：每个模型都检查了什么？

107
00:09:06,670 --> 00:09:15,728
Brian: Whether it loaded, ran forward and backward passes, compiled, and used device operators or fell back to the host processor.
Brian：检查能否加载、执行前向和反向计算、完成编译，以及使用设备算子还是回退到主机处理器。

108
00:09:15,728 --> 00:09:19,908
They used reduced but representative configurations.
他们采用了缩小规模但有代表性的配置。

109
00:09:19,908 --> 00:09:23,450
Ava: Does the article say every model passed?
Ava：文章说所有模型都通过了吗？

110
00:09:23,450 --> 00:09:26,982
Brian: No. It says the team is closing remaining gaps.
Brian：没有。文章说团队还在补齐剩余缺口。

111
00:09:26,982 --> 00:09:34,696
The supplied text refers to a graph of sweep results, but doesn't give a pass percentage to quote.
所提供的文本提到一张测试结果图，但没有给出可引用的通过率。

112
00:09:34,696 --> 00:09:38,142
Ava: Was there testing against PyTorch itself?
Ava：有没有针对 PyTorch 本身进行测试？

113
00:09:38,142 --> 00:09:38,437
Brian: Yes.
Brian：有。

114
00:09:38,437 --> 00:09:45,652
The August twenty twenty-six run had about one hundred fifty-four thousand upstream test outcomes.
2026年8月那轮测试产生了约15.4万项上游测试结果。

115
00:09:45,652 --> 00:09:50,420
Roughly sixty-nine thousand were skipped for stated reasons.
其中约6.9万项因注明的原因被跳过。

116
00:09:50,420 --> 00:09:54,322
Ava: That's a large skipped group. What was in it?
Ava：跳过的数量很大，具体包括什么？

117
00:09:54,322 --> 00:09:58,713
Brian: About thirty thousand came from PyTorch's own eligibility checks.
Brian：约3万项是由 PyTorch 自身的适用性检查跳过的。

118
00:09:58,713 --> 00:10:08,184
Around thirty-nine thousand were deliberate Neuron skips, including about twenty-two thousand tied to missing native operator lowering.
约3.9万项是 Neuron 主动跳过的，其中约2.2万项与缺少原生算子降阶实现有关。

119
00:10:08,184 --> 00:10:12,134
Ava: So those totals alone don't tell us a pass rate.
Ava：所以光看这些总数，算不出通过率。

120
00:10:12,134 --> 00:10:12,537
Brian: Right.
Brian：没错。

121
00:10:12,537 --> 00:10:20,956
The article says it reports pass rates against enabled tests, but these counts need their skip categories to make sense.
文章说通过率是按已启用的测试计算的，但要理解这些数字，还得看跳过项的分类。

122
00:10:20,956 --> 00:10:24,042
Ava: Are there any customer performance numbers?
Ava：有客户的性能数据吗？

123
00:10:24,042 --> 00:10:37,088
Brian: Deepgram reports that, in the benchmarks it evaluated, Trainium processed about sixty percent more input audio seconds per dollar than its existing graphics processing unit, or GPU, baselines.
Brian：Deepgram 表示，在其评估的基准测试中，Trainium 每美元处理的输入音频时长比现有的图形处理器（GPU）基线多约60%。

124
00:10:37,088 --> 00:10:40,990
Ava: That's specific to Deepgram's benchmarks, then.
Ava：那这个结果仅适用于 Deepgram 的基准测试。

125
00:10:40,990 --> 00:10:41,265
Brian: Yes.
Brian：对。

126
00:10:41,265 --> 00:10:53,772
Cartesia offers a different example: it says its standard inference pipeline ran with minimal PyTorch code changes, leaving it to focus custom kernels on repeatedly used layers.
Cartesia 提供了另一个例子：它表示，标准推理流水线只需少量修改 PyTorch 代码就能运行，因此可以把自定义内核的开发重点放在反复使用的层上。

127
00:10:53,772 --> 00:10:58,010
Ava: How does this connect to libraries people already use?
Ava：这和大家已经在用的库有什么关系？

128
00:10:58,010 --> 00:11:06,771
Brian: The article describes Trainium support in TorchTitan and upstream Hugging Face libraries, including Transformers and Diffusers.
Brian：文章介绍了 TorchTitan 和 Hugging Face 上游库中的 Trainium 支持，包括 Transformers 和 Diffusers。

129
00:11:06,771 --> 00:11:10,984
Those connections are central to the compatibility goal.
这些集成是实现兼容性目标的关键。

130
00:11:10,984 --> 00:11:14,286
Ava: What's still missing in this first release?
Ava：首个版本还缺什么？

131
00:11:14,286 --> 00:11:17,085
Brian: Some operators lack native lowering.
Brian：部分算子还没有原生降阶实现。

132
00:11:17,085 --> 00:11:26,182
The explicit first-release exclusions include sparse tensors, forward-mode autograd, and dynamic and symbolic shapes.
首个版本明确不支持稀疏张量、前向模式自动微分，以及动态形状和符号形状。

133
00:11:26,182 --> 00:11:29,564
The team says it's working on coverage.
团队表示正在扩大支持范围。

134
00:11:29,564 --> 00:11:32,458
Ava: And what's next for availability?
Ava：接下来何时能用上？

135
00:11:32,458 --> 00:11:42,674
Brian: The article announces an open-source public beta under Apache two point zero for October twentieth, twenty twenty-six, with installable packages.
Brian：文章宣布将于2026年10月20日推出采用 Apache 2.0 许可证的开源公测版，并提供可安装的软件包。

136
00:11:42,674 --> 00:11:47,040
It also says TorchAO and FP8 are in private beta for now.
文章还说，TorchAO 和 FP8 目前处于封闭测试阶段。

137
00:11:47,040 --> 00:11:49,790
Ava: Give me the three things to remember.
Ava：说说最值得记住的三点。

138
00:11:49,790 --> 00:11:54,412
Brian: First, TorchNeuron makes Trainium a native PyTorch device.
Brian：第一，TorchNeuron 让 Trainium 成为 PyTorch 原生设备。

139
00:11:54,412 --> 00:12:01,265
Second, eager, compile, custom kernels, and profiling give teams several ways to work.
第二，即时执行、编译、自定义内核和性能分析，为团队提供了多种工作方式。

140
00:12:01,265 --> 00:12:06,316
Third, the testing is broad, but first-release gaps remain.
第三，测试覆盖面很广，但首个版本仍有缺口。

141
00:12:06,316 --> 00:12:10,818
Ava: That's our tour of TorchNeuron. Thanks for listening.
Ava：以上就是我们对 TorchNeuron 的介绍。感谢收听。
